concepts.content:
All the different constraints described previously can be written as an overdetermined system of linear equations. Each equation has the form:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{a}_i \mathbf{Y} = b_i
\end{aligned}$$

where $\mathbf{Y}$ is a vectorized version of the image $Y$ (i.e., all rows of pixels concatenated into a flat vector). There may be many more equations than image pixels.

We translate all constraints into this form. For example, if index $i$ corresponds to a pixel inside a planar face, there will be three equations. One planarity constraint can be written as $\mathbf{a}_i = [0, \dots, 0, -1, 2, -1, 0, \dots, 0]$, $b_i=0$, with analogous equations for the others.

We solve the system by minimizing the cost function:

$$\begin{aligned}
J = \sum_i (\mathbf{a}_i\mathbf{Y} - b_i)^2
\end{aligned}$$

If some constraints are more important, weights $w_i$ can be added:

$$\begin{aligned}
J = \sum_i w_i (\mathbf{a}_i \mathbf{Y} - b_i)^2
\end{aligned}$$

This formulation results in a large system of linear constraints (more equations than pixels). Writing in matrix form:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{A} \mathbf{Y}  = \mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}$$

where row $i$ of matrix $\mathbf{A}$ contains coefficients $\mathbf{a}_i$. The system is overdetermined ($\mathbf{A}$ has more rows than columns). We compute the solution using the pseudoinverse:

$$\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{Y} = (\mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{A})^{-1} \mathbf{A}^T \mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}$$

